-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 35 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial threshold application to discovered misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bridgeview Electronics, the auditor sets overall materiality at $200,000 and performance materiality at $150,000. When a $12,000 misstatement is discovered in inventory pricing, the auditor must first determine whether this misstatement exceeds the amount the auditor considers:', '["Clearly trivial, below which misstatements need not be accumulated", "Performance materiality, which determines the scope of substantive procedures", "Overall materiality, which affects the audit opinion directly", "Tolerable misstatement for the inventory account balance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor establishes an amount below which misstatements are clearly trivial and need not be accumulated. The first step for any identified misstatement is to compare it against this clearly trivial threshold, rather than against performance materiality (choice B), which determines the scope of audit procedures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Purpose of summary of audit differences schedule
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While completing the audit of Redstone Logistics, the engagement team maintains a schedule listing all identified misstatements totaling $47,000 against overall materiality of $300,000. Under AU-C 450, the primary purpose of accumulating identified misstatements on a summary schedule is to:', '["Provide evidence that the auditor tested all material account balances", "Enable the auditor to evaluate whether uncorrected misstatements are material individually or in the aggregate", "Document that management acknowledged responsibility for all errors", "Calculate the projected misstatement for each population tested"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.06, the auditor accumulates misstatements identified during the audit to evaluate them individually and in the aggregate. The purpose is to facilitate a holistic assessment of financial statement fairness, whereas choice A incorrectly limits the purpose to merely documenting the scope of testing performed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Definition of control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, a control deficiency exists when:', '["An internal control fails to prevent all misstatements in the financial statements", "Management has not implemented an internal audit function", "A control is designed, implemented, or operated such that it cannot prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis", "The auditor identifies any error during substantive testing procedures"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a control deficiency exists when the design or operation of a control does not allow management or employees to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis. However, choice A is incorrect because a control need not prevent all misstatements — the standard focuses on timely prevention or detection capability.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Misstatement below clearly trivial threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Summit Ridge Distributors, the auditor establishes overall materiality at $500,000 and designates amounts below $15,000 as clearly trivial. The auditor discovers a $13,500 misstatement in prepaid expenses. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Immediately report the misstatement to those charged with governance", "Accumulate the misstatement on the summary of audit differences", "Disregard the misstatement since it is immaterial to the financial statements", "Not accumulate the misstatement because it falls below the clearly trivial threshold"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.05, misstatements that are clearly trivial — meaning clearly inconsequential individually or in the aggregate — need not be accumulated. At $13,500, the amount falls below the $15,000 clearly trivial threshold. In contrast, choice B would require accumulation, which is unnecessary for amounts below this threshold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluating interrelated control deficiencies as combined material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Health Systems, the auditor identifies three control deficiencies: (1) the purchasing manager can approve and record transactions without independent review, (2) no automated three-way matching exists for vendor invoices, and (3) monthly account reconciliations for accounts payable are not performed. Each is individually assessed as a significant deficiency. Under AU-C 265, when evaluating whether these interrelated deficiencies constitute a material weakness, the auditor should primarily consider:', '["Whether the combination of deficiencies creates a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement would not be prevented or detected on a timely basis", "Whether each deficiency was caused by the same root cause within the organization", "Whether the total dollar amount of transactions processed through all three controls exceeds materiality", "Whether management has acknowledged each deficiency in a written representation letter"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A13, the auditor considers whether individually less severe deficiencies, when combined, represent a material weakness. The key criterion is whether the combination creates a reasonable possibility of an undetected material misstatement, rather than root cause analysis (choice B), which is relevant to remediation but not the primary severity criterion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — Identifying a classification misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cedar Point Furnishings, the engagement team discovers that $45,000 of long-term debt maturing within twelve months was not reclassified to current liabilities on the balance sheet. This type of error is best described as:', '["A factual misstatement arising from an incorrect accounting estimate", "A classification misstatement that affects the presentation of the financial statements", "A projected misstatement from the sampling population", "A judgmental misstatement based on an unreasonable management estimate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.04, misstatements include classification misstatements that cause financial statement items to be presented or disclosed incorrectly. The debt reclassification error affects presentation between current and noncurrent liabilities. Unlike choice A, which involves a definite error in an account balance, a classification misstatement involves incorrect presentation or disclosure.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Response when misstatement indicates fraud
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakewood Manufacturing, the auditor discovers a $28,000 misstatement in revenue caused by a sales manager manually overriding the system to record fictitious sales near quarter-end. Materiality is set at $400,000. Under AU-C 240 and AU-C 450, the auditor''s most appropriate response is to:', '["Accumulate the misstatement and request management correction since it is below materiality", "Communicate the misstatement to those charged with governance at the end of the audit", "Reconsider the assessment of fraud risk and evaluate the implications for other aspects of the audit", "Issue a qualified opinion because the misstatement resulted from intentional override"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 240.36, when the auditor identifies a misstatement resulting from fraud or suspected fraud, the auditor should reconsider the fraud risk assessment and its implications for other audit areas regardless of the amount. Although the misstatement is below materiality, choice A is incorrect because simply accumulating a fraud-related misstatement ignores the broader audit implications.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Iron curtain vs rollover approach for prior-period misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Westfield Retail Corp., the auditor identifies a $35,000 overstatement of depreciation expense originating in the prior year that was not corrected. The current year''s income statement is fairly stated on its own, but the balance sheet carries the $35,000 cumulative error. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate the prior-period misstatement by considering:', '["Only the income statement effect in the current year since the error originated previously", "Only the balance sheet effect since the income statement is correctly stated", "The misstatement solely based on whether it exceeds performance materiality", "Both the income statement and balance sheet effects to assess whether the financial statements as a whole are materially misstated"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A17, the auditor considers both the rollover effect (current-year income statement impact) and the iron curtain effect (cumulative balance sheet impact) when evaluating prior-period misstatements. However, choices A and B are each incomplete because evaluating only one financial statement fails to provide a comprehensive assessment of material misstatement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Prior-period uncorrected misstatements in current year evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While planning the audit of Northern Star Energy for the year ended December 31, 2025, the auditor reviews last year''s summary of uncorrected misstatements totaling $42,000 against materiality of $300,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should treat these prior-period uncorrected misstatements by:', '["Including them in the current year''s evaluation of whether uncorrected misstatements are material in the aggregate", "Ignoring them because they were evaluated and deemed immaterial in the prior period", "Requiring management to restate the prior-period financial statements before continuing", "Reporting them as a significant deficiency to those charged with governance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.10, the auditor includes the effect of prior-period uncorrected misstatements on the current-period financial statements when evaluating aggregate materiality. Although these misstatements were deemed immaterial in the prior period, choice B is incorrect because their cumulative effect could become material over time.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — Aggregate misstatements approaching materiality across accounts
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Granite Peak Insurance, the auditor discovers misstatements totaling $175,000 scattered across revenue, claims expense, and investment income. Overall materiality is $200,000. While each individual account misstatement is below its tolerable misstatement, the aggregate approaches overall materiality. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Accept the financial statements because no single account exceeds its tolerable misstatement", "Request management to correct the misstatements or consider modifying the audit opinion if correction is refused", "Increase materiality to accommodate the spread of misstatements across multiple accounts", "Issue an adverse opinion because misstatements affecting multiple accounts are automatically pervasive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.11, when aggregate uncorrected misstatements approach materiality, the auditor should request correction or perform additional procedures to reduce the risk. However, choice A is incorrect because accepting based on individual account assessments ignores the aggregate effect, which is the auditor''s primary evaluation criterion.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Communication of deficiencies less severe than significant
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Silverline Technologies, the auditor identifies several control deficiencies individually less severe than a significant deficiency. Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s communication obligation for these deficiencies is to:', '["Include them in the same written communication as significant deficiencies and material weaknesses", "Report them to those charged with governance in a formal presentation", "Communicate them to management, either orally or in writing, unless clearly inconsequential", "Document them only in the working papers with no obligation to communicate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor should communicate in writing or orally to management control deficiencies that merit attention, even if they are not significant deficiencies. In contrast, choice D incorrectly suggests no communication obligation exists, whereas the auditor must inform management of improvable control matters.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial threshold definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the term "clearly trivial" is best described as:', '["An amount equal to five percent of overall materiality", "Any misstatement that management considers immaterial", "The threshold below which misstatements need not be reported to those charged with governance", "An amount below which misstatements would clearly be inconsequential whether taken individually or in the aggregate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial is not simply another expression for "not material" — it is a smaller amount that the auditor designates as clearly inconsequential. Unlike choice A, there is no fixed percentage rule; rather than applying a formula, the auditor uses professional judgment to establish this threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors in misstatement evaluation (management intent)
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Horizon Pharmaceuticals, the auditor discovers that management intentionally understated a $15,000 accrued liability to convert a reported net loss to net income. Overall materiality is $500,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Treat the misstatement as material because the intentional nature and effect on earnings trend represent qualitative factors that override the quantitative assessment", "Classify the misstatement as clearly trivial because it is far below materiality", "Accumulate the misstatement without special consideration because it does not exceed materiality", "Issue a disclaimer of opinion due to management''s intentional actions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.11 and .A20, the auditor considers qualitative factors including whether a misstatement changes a loss to income or involves intentional acts. Although quantitatively below materiality, the $15,000 amount may be qualitatively material. In contrast, choice B incorrectly classifies it as clearly trivial, ignoring these qualitative factors entirely.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Timing of material weakness communication
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During interim testing at Riverbend Manufacturing, the auditor identifies a material weakness in revenue recognition controls. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should communicate this material weakness to those charged with governance:', '["Only after completing all audit fieldwork and issuing the audit report", "In writing on a timely basis, which may be during the audit rather than waiting until completion", "Orally during the next scheduled audit committee meeting", "Within 30 calendar days of the discovery as specified by auditing standards"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.10, the auditor communicates significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing on a timely basis. A timely communication may occur during the audit rather than after completion. However, choice D is incorrect because there is no prescribed 30-day deadline; the standard requires timeliness based on circumstances.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Definition of pervasive
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In auditing standards, the term "pervasive" in relation to misstatements means:', '["The misstatement exceeds overall materiality for the financial statements", "Multiple misstatements exist in a single account balance", "The effects are not confined to specific elements and, if confined, represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements", "The misstatement was caused by a systemic failure in internal controls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 705.06, pervasive effects are those not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items, or if confined, represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements. Although exceeding materiality (choice A) determines whether a misstatement is material, it does not alone establish pervasiveness, which requires a broader assessment of spread and impact.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Auditor consideration of corrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, after management corrects all identified misstatements, the auditor should:', '["Issue an unmodified opinion without further consideration", "Remove the corrected misstatements from the audit documentation", "Determine whether overall materiality needs to be revised upward", "Consider whether the nature and circumstances of the corrected misstatements indicate additional risks of material misstatement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.08, even after management corrects identified misstatements, the auditor should evaluate their nature and circumstances to determine whether they indicate additional risks such as systemic errors or potential fraud. However, choice A is incorrect because issuing an opinion without further consideration ignores the auditor''s responsibility to assess whether corrected misstatements signal broader risks.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Deficiency in operation definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, a deficiency in the operation of a control exists when:', '["A properly designed control does not operate effectively or the person performing the control lacks the authority or competence to do so", "The entity has not established written policies and procedures for the control", "Management has not documented the purpose and design of the control", "The control was implemented after the fiscal year end under audit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.07, an operating deficiency exists when a well-designed control does not operate as designed or the person performing the control lacks the necessary competence or authority. In contrast, choice B describes the absence of written policies, which relates to design rather than operation, as a control can operate effectively without formal documentation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Design deficiency vs. operating deficiency distinction
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cascade Financial Group, the auditor discovers that the entity''s journal entry control requires supervisory review only for entries exceeding $10,000, with no control for entries below that threshold. A $9,500 fraudulent journal entry was posted undetected. Under AU-C 265, this scenario most likely represents:', '["A deficiency in operation because the existing control failed to detect the entry", "A deficiency in design because no control was designed to address journal entries below the $10,000 threshold", "Neither a design nor an operational deficiency because the threshold was set by management", "A compensating control failure that should be evaluated separately from the journal entry process"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A3, a design deficiency exists when a necessary control is missing or not suitably designed to meet the control objective. Since no control addresses entries below $10,000, this is a gap in design. Unlike choice A, which would apply if the supervisory review existed but was not performed, this situation involves a missing control rather than a failed one.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Qualified vs. adverse opinion determination
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Sterling Enterprises, the auditor identifies $350,000 of uncorrected misstatements in inventory representing 8% of total assets and 25% of net income. Management refuses correction. Overall materiality is $200,000. Under AU-C 705, the auditor must determine whether to issue a qualified or adverse opinion by evaluating whether the misstatement is:', '["Greater than twice the overall materiality threshold, which automatically triggers an adverse opinion", "Likely to influence the economic decisions of all financial statement users", "Material and pervasive to the financial statements as a whole", "Caused by management''s intentional refusal rather than a disagreement over accounting treatment"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 705.08, the auditor issues an adverse opinion when misstatements are both material and pervasive, whereas a qualified opinion is appropriate when misstatements are material but not pervasive. However, choice A is incorrect because there is no automatic two-times-materiality threshold; the auditor evaluates pervasiveness based on the breadth and significance of the effect.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Uncorrected misstatement definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, an uncorrected misstatement is defined as:', '["Any error discovered during the audit that exceeds performance materiality", "A misstatement that management disputes as being an error", "An error identified during the prior-year audit that was not corrected in the current year", "A misstatement that the auditor has identified during the audit and that has not been corrected"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.04, an uncorrected misstatement is simply a misstatement the auditor identified during the audit that management has not corrected. Unlike choice A, the misstatement does not need to exceed performance materiality — even small misstatements below materiality are considered uncorrected if they remain in the financial statements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Systemic control failures across subsidiaries in group audit
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the group audit of Atlas Global Enterprises, the auditor identifies that three of eight subsidiaries share the same control deficiency: bank reconciliations are not reviewed by an independent party. Each subsidiary''s unreviewed reconciliation covers between $2 million and $5 million in cash balances. Group materiality is $1.5 million. Under AU-C 265, when evaluating the severity of this systemic deficiency, the auditor should:', '["Evaluate the aggregate exposure across all affected subsidiaries because the systemic nature increases the reasonable possibility of a material misstatement", "Assess each subsidiary''s deficiency independently based on its own component materiality", "Classify the deficiency as a significant deficiency because no actual misstatement has been identified", "Defer evaluation until the consolidation process reveals whether any misstatements actually occurred"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor considers the magnitude of the potential misstatement and likelihood of occurrence. When the same deficiency exists across multiple subsidiaries, the aggregate exposure exceeds group materiality. In contrast, choice B is incorrect because assessing each subsidiary independently understates the combined risk that the systemic deficiency poses to the consolidated financial statements.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Distinguishing significant deficiency from material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Rim Logistics, the auditor discovers that the accounts receivable clerk can both create customer accounts and process cash receipts without independent oversight. Testing found no errors, but the clerk has access to $3 million in annual receivables. Materiality is $250,000. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should classify this deficiency by considering:', '["Only the results of substantive testing, since no actual misstatement was identified", "The magnitude of the potential misstatement that could result from the deficiency and the likelihood of occurrence", "Whether the deficiency was previously communicated to management in a prior audit", "The total annual revenue of the company relative to the exposure amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, the severity of a control deficiency depends on the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the likelihood it could occur — not whether a misstatement actually occurred. However, choice A is incorrect because relying solely on substantive testing results ignores the prospective risk that the segregation-of-duties weakness could enable a material misstatement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Combined deficiencies in integrated audit context
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the integrated audit of Vanguard Aerospace, the auditor identifies deficiencies in inventory count procedures, cost allocation methods, and physical access to the warehouse. Individually, each is a significant deficiency. Historical data shows inventory misstatements in this industry frequently require restatement. Under PCAOB AS 2201 and AU-C 265, the auditor''s evaluation of these combined deficiencies should:', '["Focus on whether management has developed a remediation plan for each individual deficiency", "Consider only the deficiencies that directly affected financial statement amounts during the current year", "Assess whether the combination creates a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement in inventory could occur and not be prevented or detected on a timely basis", "Automatically classify them as a material weakness because all three deficiencies relate to the same account"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A13 and PCAOB AS 2201.62, individual deficiencies that are not material weaknesses may, in combination, represent a material weakness. The auditor evaluates the combined effect on detection and prevention capability. Unlike choice D, which assumes automatic classification, the combination must be assessed based on whether it actually creates a reasonable possibility of undetected material misstatement rather than simply counting related deficiencies.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Documentation requirement for identified misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, the auditor is required to document:', '["Only misstatements that exceed the overall materiality threshold", "Misstatements communicated to those charged with governance only", "Only uncorrected misstatements that remain in the financial statements", "All misstatements accumulated during the audit and whether they have been corrected"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor documents all accumulated misstatements and notes whether they were corrected by management. This documentation supports the auditor''s evaluation and provides evidence for the audit file. In contrast, choice A would fail to capture the aggregate picture needed for proper evaluation by limiting documentation to amounts exceeding materiality.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Management bias in accounting estimates
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Crestwood Industries, the auditor observes that management consistently selects accounting estimates at the aggressive end of the acceptable range across revenue recognition, warranty reserves, and depreciation. While each individual estimate is acceptable, the cumulative effect understates expenses by $180,000. Overall materiality is $250,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate this pattern by:', '["Considering whether the cumulative effect of management''s estimate selections indicates a possible bias that creates a risk of material misstatement", "Accepting each estimate individually since all fall within the acceptable range", "Requiring management to change at least one estimate to the midpoint of the range", "Disclosing the pattern in the auditor''s report as an emphasis-of-matter paragraph"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A21, the auditor considers whether differences between management''s estimates and the auditor''s judgment, taken together, suggest possible management bias. Although each estimate is individually reasonable, choice B is incorrect because accepting each in isolation ignores the auditor''s obligation to evaluate the cumulative directional effect that may indicate bias.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Remediation effect on prior-year material weakness classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Harbor Point Financial, the auditor identified a material weakness in loan approval controls in the prior year. Management implemented new approval procedures and trained staff during the current year. Under AU-C 265, when determining whether to continue classifying this as a material weakness, the auditor should:', '["Automatically downgrade the classification to a significant deficiency after one year of remediation", "Test the operating effectiveness of the remediated control before changing the classification", "Accept management''s assertion that the weakness has been resolved without additional testing", "Maintain the material weakness classification for a minimum of two consecutive audit periods"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, the auditor must obtain evidence that the new or revised control operates effectively before changing a deficiency classification. Implementing remediation alone is insufficient. Rather than automatically downgrading after one year (choice A), the auditor must verify through testing that the remediated control works as designed.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Magnitude concept in control deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cornerstone Building Supply, the auditor discovers that the payroll system lacks a control to verify overtime calculations for hourly employees. The entity has 200 hourly employees with total annual overtime pay of $1.2 million. Overall materiality is $400,000. Under AU-C 265, when assessing the magnitude of the potential misstatement from this deficiency, the auditor should consider:', '["Only the misstatements actually detected during substantive payroll testing", "The total payroll expense as the maximum potential misstatement", "The amount of overtime transactions that could be misstated given the nature of the missing control", "The number of employees affected rather than the dollar volume of transactions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, magnitude refers to the potential misstatement amount that could result from the deficiency. The relevant population is the $1.2 million in overtime affected by the missing control, rather than total payroll (choice B). However, choice A is also incorrect because limiting the assessment to detected misstatements understates the prospective risk of uncontrolled overtime calculations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — IT general control deficiency affecting automated application controls
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Data Corp., the auditor determines that the IT general control over user access management is defective — terminated employees retained system access for an average of 45 days after departure. The financial application processes $50 million annually and relies on automated controls for transaction authorization. Under AU-C 265 and AU-C 315, when evaluating this IT deficiency, the auditor should:', '["Classify the deficiency based solely on whether unauthorized transactions were actually detected", "Limit the evaluation to the IT general control without considering its effect on application controls", "Treat the deficiency as a significant deficiency because it relates to IT rather than a financial process", "Evaluate whether the defective IT general control undermines the effectiveness of dependent automated application controls, potentially creating a material weakness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 315.A65 and AU-C 265.A14, IT general controls support the continued effective functioning of automated application controls. When user access controls are defective, the integrity of dependent automated controls is compromised. Unlike choice B, which limits the evaluation to the IT control itself, the auditor must consider the cascading effect on application-level controls that rely on proper access management.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Deficiency in design definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, a deficiency in the design of a control exists when:', '["A control necessary to meet a control objective is missing or not suitably designed to prevent or detect misstatements on a timely basis", "An existing control fails to operate as designed due to human error", "Management has documented the control but has not yet implemented it", "The control has been in place for less than one fiscal year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.07, a design deficiency exists when a control necessary to meet a control objective is missing or is not suitably designed so that even if it operates as designed, the objective would not be met. In contrast, choice B describes a deficiency in operation rather than design, as the control exists but fails due to human error.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Reasonable possibility standard in deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Eastgate Distributors, the auditor identifies a control deficiency in the accounts payable duplicate payment detection process. Under AU-C 265, when evaluating whether this deficiency is a significant deficiency or material weakness, the concept of "reasonable possibility" means:', '["A probability of occurrence greater than 75 percent", "A virtual certainty that a misstatement will occur within the next fiscal year", "The chance of a future event occurring is more than remote, meaning it is reasonably possible or probable", "A quantifiable statistical probability determined through sampling procedures"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A10, reasonable possibility encompasses both "reasonably possible" and "probable" as defined in ASC 450 — essentially any likelihood greater than remote. Unlike choice A, there is no 75 percent threshold; rather than a specific quantified probability, the standard uses a qualitative assessment that a misstatement could occur.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
